    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the build is green. Here's a summary of what I changed to make `householdId` deterministic.\n\n## What changed\n\n**`OwnerDerivations.householdId`** \u2014 replaced the old address-based `UUID.nameUUIDFromBytes` derivation with the spec's formula: the first 12 lower-case hex chars of `SHA-256(normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode)`. Dropped the now-unused `UUID` import.\n\n**`Owner.java`**\n- `computeHouseholdId()` now derives from `normalize(lastName)` + `postcode` (via the new `OwnerDerivations.householdId(String, String)`).\n- `sameHouseholdAs(other)` now means \"same last name **and postcode**\" (was last name + address), so it matches the new household key.\n- Removed the obsolete address-based `householdKey()` method.\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 reworked the household flow:\n- The `householdId` is now computed and set on **every** owner unconditionally (it's deterministic).\n- `sharesHousehold` no longer *creates* the link \u2014 it only **bypasses the duplicate block**: if an existing owner is in the same household and `sharesHousehold` is not set \u2192 **409**; if it is set \u2192 the owner is created as a **declared member**.\n- A declared member is **not** flagged as a possible duplicate (`assignPossibleDuplicate` short-circuits to `false`/`null` for them).\n- Removed the now-redundant `identityKeyAlreadyUsed` 409 check (cross-household owners can never collide on the household-keyed identity key, and same-household collisions are handled by the new block) and the obsolete `joinHousehold` method.\n- `householdSize` keeps keying off the computed `householdId`.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `sharesHousehold`, `householdId`, `identityKey`, and `possibleDuplicate` descriptions to match the new behaviour.\n\n## Result\n\nThe new acceptance test (`coreSameLastNameAndPostcodeShareHouseholdId`) passes: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**. Same last name + postcode share a `householdId` (the second owner admitted via `sharesHousehold`), and a different last name yields a different `householdId`.\n\nOne behavioural note worth flagging: because the household is now keyed on `(lastName, postcode)`, two owners with the same last name and **no** postcode resolve to the same household (empty postcode component), so the second would be a 409 unless it sets `sharesHousehold`. No test exercises that case, and it follows directly from the spec's \"keyed on (lastName, postcode)\" wording.",
